.\" bedrock(1) man page
.\" Section 1: user commands.
.\" Source: man/bedrock.1 in the bedrock repository.
.TH BEDROCK 1 "2026-09-02" "bedrock 1.2.3" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bedrock \- CLI auditor for fundamental domain security controls (DNS, DNSSEC, Email, BIMI, Web/TLS)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bedrock
.RI [ flags ]
.I domain
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bedrock
inspects a target domain against IETF and vendor (Gmail) requirements
across DNS, DNSSEC, Email, and Web/TLS
and emits a verdict per check.
BIMI checks (BIMI Group draft + Gmail requirements) are reported under
the Email category.
Each result cites the RFC section that defines the requirement,
and every failure is accompanied by a copy-pasteable record, header,
or configuration snippet the operator can publish to fix it.
.PP
The tool is a single static binary with no runtime dependencies,
suitable for CI pipelines and ad-hoc audits.
By default it performs active probes
(HTTPS GETs, SMTP STARTTLS handshakes, MTA-STS and BIMI VMC fetches);
pass
.B \-\-no\-active
to confine the run to DNS lookups.
.PP
Every probe connection (HTTP, TLS, SMTP, AXFR and QUIC) refuses denylisted
RFC 1918, loopback, link-local, ULA, CGNAT, multicast, reserved, and
cloud-metadata addresses to neutralise SSRF via attacker-influenced DNS.
A resolver given as an IP address in that space is rejected too; one given
by name is checked only when DoH dials it, since UDP and DoT dial the name
unchecked.
These checks apply unless the
.B BEDROCK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_RESOLVER
environment variable is set to a non-empty value (intended for hermetic
test labs).
.PP
.I domain
may be an internationalised domain name; it is normalised to ASCII
(Punycode), lowercased, and any trailing dot is stripped.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version line and exit.
.TP
.B \-\-json
Write the JSON report even when standard output is a terminal.
This flag has no config-file key.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-color
Print the terminal report without colour.
Colour is also off when
.B NO_COLOR
is set to a non-empty value or
.B TERM
is
.BR dumb .
JSON output is never coloured.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-active
Skip active probes.
No SMTP STARTTLS handshakes, no HTTPS GETs, no MTA-STS policy fetch,
no BIMI VMC fetch, no QUIC dials.
Checks that require a connection are reported as
.B N/A
so the result schema remains stable.
.TP
.BI \-\-resolver " spec"
Use the specified recursive DNS resolver. Accepted forms:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
.B host
or
.B host:port
\(em UDP plaintext; an IPv6 address takes a port only in brackets
.RB ( [2001:db8::53]:5353 )
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cloudflare ", " google ", " quad9 ", " opendns
\(em named preset over UDP
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cloudflare\-dot ", " google\-dot ", " quad9\-dot ", " opendns\-dot
\(em named preset over DNS-over-TLS (port 853)
.IP \(bu 2
.BR cloudflare\-doh ", " google\-doh ", " quad9\-doh ", " opendns\-doh
\(em named preset over DNS-over-HTTPS (RFC 8484)
.IP \(bu 2
.BI tls:// host[:port]
\(em explicit DoT to a custom server, named by DNS name rather than IP address
.IP \(bu 2
.BI https:// host/path
\(em explicit DoH to a custom server
.RE
.TP
.BI \-\-resolvers " csv"
Comma-separated list of resolver specs (same syntax as
.BR \-\-resolver ).
The first resolver serves every lookup; the
.B dnssec.sentinel
check (RFC 8509) tests the root trust anchors of each listed resolver
separately.
Preferred over
.B \-\-resolver
when both are supplied.
.TP
.BI \-\-timeout " duration"
Per-operation timeout for DNS queries, HTTPS GETs, and TLS handshakes.
Accepts any Go duration string greater than zero
.RB ( 500ms ", " 5s ", " 1m ).
Default:
.BR 5s .
A
.I timeout
in the config file that does not parse gets a warning on standard error,
and the scan uses the default.
.TP
.BI \-\-config " path"
Read defaults from a JSON config file at
.IR path .
Keys mirror the long-form flag names with hyphens replaced by underscores.
Flag values supplied on the command line override config values;
either resolver flag overrides both the
.B resolver
and
.B resolvers
keys.
.TP
.BI \-\-only " csv"
Comma-separated list of categories to run
(e.g.\&
.BR Email,WWW ).
The categories, matched case-insensitively, are
.BR DNS ", " DNSSEC ", " Email ", " Subdomain ", and " WWW ;
any other name is a usage error.
Checks in other categories do not run.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " csv"
Comma-separated list of categories to skip, from the same set as
.BR \-\-only ;
their checks do not run.
.TP
.BI \-\-ids " csv"
Comma-separated list of specific check IDs to include
(e.g.\&
.BR email.dmarc.record,web.hsts ).
The run-level results
.B dns.resolver.unreachable
and
.BI registry.panic. id
are always included.
A warning on standard error names any entry that matches no result.
.TP
.BI \-\-severity " level"
Minimum severity to include in output. One of
.BR info ", " pass ", " warn ", " fail .
.B N/A
results are always shown so the schema remains stable.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-ct
Enable Certificate Transparency lookups via the crt.sh search API
(third-party). Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-enable\-rbl
Enable DNSBL/RBL queries against Spamhaus, Barracuda, SpamCop, SORBS,
and Surriel PSBL. Listings are reported as
.B WARN ,
not
.B FAIL ,
since false positives and stale entries are common. Off by default.
.TP
.B \-\-subdomains
Enumerate subdomains via passive third-party sources
.RB ( hackertarget ,
.BR anubis ,
.BR threatcrowd ,
.BR wayback )
and probe each discovered host for TLS reachability and certificate
hygiene. These providers are external services with varying coverage and
freshness, and querying them may disclose the domains under investigation.
Hostnames are allowlisted against
.B ^[a-zA-Z0-9._-]+$
at both source and enumerate time; the underscore is permitted so service
labels such as
.B _dmarc
are not discarded (an injection-safety filter, not an RFC 1123 hostname
validator). Off by default.
.TP
.BI \-\-baseline " path"
Path to a previously-saved JSON report. Results are diffed against the
baseline and any check that is now
.B FAIL
but was previously
.BR PASS ", " WARN ", or " INFO
is reported as a regression. Duplicate IDs in a baseline file cause every
current
.B FAIL
for that ID to be reported as a regression (fails closed \(em an ambiguous
baseline cannot mask a regression).
.TP
.B \-\-regression\-only
Requires
.BR \-\-baseline :
exit non-zero only on NEW failures versus the baseline.
Pre-existing failures that have not changed are ignored.
Useful as a CI gate that won't fail on legacy issues you've consciously
deferred.
.SH OUTPUT
The format depends on where standard output goes:
a terminal gets a text report;
a pipe, a file, or
.B \-\-json
gets one JSON document.
A program that reads the output through a pseudo-terminal, as with
.BR "ssh \-t" ,
.BR "docker run \-t" ,
.BR script (1)
or
.BR expect (1),
therefore gets the text report.
Better not to allocate one
.RB ( "ssh \-T" ,
or
.B docker run
without
.BR \-t ):
standard output is then a pipe, which gets JSON and no progress lines.
Where a pseudo-terminal cannot be avoided, pass
.B \-\-json
and keep standard error, which shares the terminal, out of the stream
.RB ( "2>/dev/null"
inside the session), or set
.BR CI=1 .
.SS Terminal report
The report runs from least to most important, so the last screen holds
the fixes and the result:
a header;
checks that did not run or do not apply, grouped by reason;
one line per
.B PASS
and
.B INFO
result;
each
.B WARN
and
.B FAIL
in full, with its evidence, references, and fix;
with
.BR \-\-baseline ,
the new failures;
a summary per category;
and a verdict that gives the exit status.
Each fix follows a
.RI \(lqfix\ ( N \ lines):\(rq
label and is printed unindented, so most fixes paste as-is.
.PP
Colour marks only
.BR bedrock 's
own words: status words, section headings, non-zero
.B FAIL
and
.B WARN
counts, and the verdict.
.BR \-\-no\-color ,
a non-empty
.BR NO_COLOR ,
or
.B TERM=dumb
turns it off.
.SS JSON
The schema is
.RI { target ", " results "[], " summary } ,
where each result has
.IR id ", " category ", " title ", " status ", " evidence ", " remediation ", " rfc_refs .
The
.I summary
object totals the rendered results per category and per status. It is
computed after the filtering options, so totals always match the
.I results
array.
With
.BR \-\-baseline ,
a
.I regressions
array lists the
.I id
and
.I title
of each new
.BR FAIL ;
it is omitted when there are none.
.SS Standard error
While a scan runs, standard error gets progress lines that are appended,
never redrawn: a start line, a heartbeat after 3 seconds and then every
10 seconds naming up to two checks not done yet (running, or queued for a
worker), and a line on interrupt.
They appear only when standard error is a terminal,
.B CI
is unset or empty, and standard output is a terminal or a regular file;
never with a pipe.
When those conditions hold and standard output is a regular file,
standard error also ends with the verdict, the checks an interrupt cut
short, and up to ten failing IDs.
.PP
SIGINT or SIGTERM stops the scan and prints the partial report.
The exit status follows the results it holds, which can include a
.B FAIL
from a check the interrupt cut short.
The terminal report says
.B INCOMPLETE
and lists the checks that did not finish, by check name:
a check can report its results under other IDs, and the results a
cut-short check did report may reflect the interrupt rather than the
target.
With
.B \-\-only
or
.BR \-\-exclude ,
the list keeps to the categories the report shows.
The JSON has no such marker, so standard error always says
.BR INCOMPLETE :
in the verdict when progress is on, otherwise in a single verdict line.
A second SIGINT ends
.B bedrock
at once, without a report.
.PP
Warnings and errors also go to standard error, one line each, whatever it
is: an
.B \-\-ids
entry that matches no result (not checked after an interrupt)
and a config
.I timeout
that does not parse.
To parse the JSON, keep standard error out of the stream.
.SS Untrusted text
In both formats, every C0 control character except TAB, every C1 control
character, and DEL in the report's text is replaced with U+FFFD;
only a fix keeps its line breaks, normalised to LF.
Data from the target therefore cannot carry terminal escape sequences.
The terminal report also turns TAB into a space and shows as
.B \euXXXX
.RB ( \eUXXXXXXXX
above U+FFFF) every character that is not graphic or that renders as
nothing: format characters such as bidirectional overrides and zero-width
spaces, line and paragraph separators, private-use and unassigned code
points, fillers, and variation selectors.
It never wraps, truncates, or colours text from the target.
Progress lines, warnings and errors on standard error get the same
treatment.
Target text that itself reads
.B \eu202E
looks the same as an escaped character; the JSON report has the exact
text.
.SH SEVERITY
Each check returns one of five statuses:
.TP
.B PASS
The check satisfied its requirement.
.TP
.B WARN
A non-fatal observation, or a probe that could not complete (a timeout,
a reset connection, an unreachable network, a temporary DNS failure such
as SERVFAIL or REFUSED, an SSRF refusal, an interrupted scan) in a check
that grades the target, whose evidence starts with
\(lqcould not determine:\(rq.
Does
.I not
affect the exit code.
An informational check whose probe could not complete stays
.B INFO
and quotes the error.
A refused connection and NXDOMAIN are answers from the target and are
graded as such.
.TP
.B FAIL
The requirement was not met. Affects the exit code.
A remediation snippet is always provided.
.TP
.B INFO
A purely informational observation (e.g.\& detected software, profile match,
legacy Google Workspace MX layout).
.TP
.B N/A
The check was not applicable to this target
(e.g.\& BIMI checks against a domain with no MX, or any active probe with
.B \-\-no\-active
in effect).
Checks of content fetched over a TLS chain that did not verify report
.B N/A
with evidence \(lqTLS chain invalid; see web.cert.*\(rq.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The report contained no
.B FAIL
results.
.B WARN
and
.B INFO
do not affect exit status.
.TP
.B 1
The report contained at least one
.B FAIL
(or, with
.BR \-\-regression\-only ,
a new
.B FAIL
versus the baseline).
A resolver that answers none of the scan's DNS queries, by sending no
reply or only errors such as SERVFAIL and REFUSED, yields the
.B dns.resolver.unreachable
.BR FAIL ,
whichever categories run.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, invalid input or configuration, or a failed write.
These include an unknown category, a malformed or denylisted resolver,
no system resolver and no
.BR \-\-resolver ,
a
.B \-\-timeout
of zero or less, and
.B \-\-regression\-only
without
.BR \-\-baseline ;
each exits before the scan starts.
.SH EXAMPLES
.TP
Basic audit; a terminal gets the text report:
.EX
$ bedrock example.org
.EE
.TP
The JSON report on a terminal:
.EX
$ bedrock \-\-json example.org
.EE
.TP
Pin a recursive resolver for reproducibility:
.EX
$ bedrock \-\-resolver 1.1.1.1:53 example.org
.EE
.TP
.RB "Filter the JSON to just failures with " jq (1):
.EX
$ bedrock example.org | \\
    jq '.results[] | select(.status == "FAIL")'
.EE
.TP
Save the JSON report; standard error on a terminal shows the verdict:
.EX
$ bedrock example.org > example.org.report.json
.EE
.TP
DNS-only audit of a third-party domain (no outbound TCP beyond DNS):
.EX
$ bedrock \-\-no\-active partner.example.com
.EE
.TP
.RB "Fail a CI job on any " FAIL ", logging only the totals:"
.EX
$ bedrock prod.example.com > posture.json || {
    status=$?
    jq \-c .summary.totals posture.json
    exit "$status"
  }
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/resolv.conf
Consulted when
.B \-\-resolver
is not supplied.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BEDROCK_ALLOW_PRIVATE_RESOLVER
When set to a non-empty value, bypasses the SSRF denylist so loopback /
RFC 1918 resolvers and probe destinations can be used. Intended for
hermetic test environments only; production callers must leave this unset.
.TP
.B CI
When set to a non-empty value, as most CI services do, turns off the
progress lines and the verdict on standard error.
.TP
.B NO_COLOR
When set to a non-empty value, turns off colour in the terminal report,
as
.B \-\-no\-color
does.
It does not select JSON; use
.B \-\-json
for that.
.TP
.B TERM
When set to
.BR dumb ,
turns off colour in the terminal report.
.PP
Otherwise
.B bedrock
ignores all environment variables other than those consulted by the Go
runtime
.RB ( SSL_CERT_FILE ", " SSL_CERT_DIR )
for system root certificates and any standard OS resolver configuration.
In particular,
.B FORCE_COLOR
and
.B CLICOLOR_FORCE
have no effect.
.SH STANDARDS
.B bedrock
checks reference, among others:
.RS
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 1034, 1035, 1912, 2181, 2308, 3596, 3597, 5936 (DNS)
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 4033, 4034, 4035, 4509, 5011, 5155, 6605, 6781, 7344, 8509, 8624 (DNSSEC)
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 5321, 3207, 7505 (SMTP / STARTTLS / Null MX)
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 7208 (SPF), 6376 (DKIM), 8463 (ed25519 DKIM),
draft-ietf-dkim-dkim2-spec (DKIM2)
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 9989 (DMARC; obsoletes 7489 and 9091), 9990 (DMARC aggregate
reporting), 9991 (DMARC failure reporting), 8020 (NXDOMAIN semantics),
8617 (ARC; Historic-track per draft-ietf-dmarc-arc-to-historic)
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 8460 (TLS-RPT), 8461 (MTA-STS), 7672 (DANE-SMTP), 5782 (DNSBL)
.IP \(bu 2
RFC 5280, 6125, 6797 (HSTS), 7525 / BCP 195 (TLS), 8659 (CAA),
6066 / 6960 (OCSP), 9162 (CT), 9113 (HTTP/2), 9114 (HTTP/3),
9116 (security.txt)
.IP \(bu 2
BIMI Group draft, Gmail BIMI requirements, RFC 3709 LogotypeExtn
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
The Go standard library does not expose the negotiated TLS elliptic curve;
TLS profile scoring uses protocol version, negotiated cipher suite, and
the served certificate's key as discriminators. Accepted EC curves are
detected via probe-and-detect in a separate check.
.PP
DKIM selector probing covers a fixed list of well-known selectors plus
ESP-specific selectors derived from SPF includes. Custom per-tenant
selectors (e.g. HubSpot's
.BR hs1\-<id>\-<domain>
pattern) cannot be discovered without the customer ID.
.PP
VMC chain validation uses
.B ExtKeyUsageAny
because the BIMI EKU OIDs
.RB ( 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.31
and
.BR 1.3.6.1.5.5.7.3.32 )
are not present in the Go standard library's root usage table. A
BIMI-specific EKU gate runs
.I before
chain verification to reject certificates lacking the correct purpose.
The logotype extension is now decoded via real RFC 3709 ASN.1
(previously a SHA-256 byte search); the SVG hash in the cert must match
the fetched SVG for the check to pass.
.SH BUGS
Report issues against the source repository.
.SH SEE ALSO
